IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g9-interpretation`

-- g9-common.l4: what Schedules 1 and 2 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 have in common, and nothing that differs between them.
-- It holds the counting of every period the two Schedules use (one public decision per period, each delegating by CONSIDER to one private function per reading)
-- and the periods of service. The FIGURES (3 months or one month, 4 weeks or 8 weeks, ...) are NOT here: each Schedule module names its own figure and passes it in,
-- so that the difference between the Schedules (trap T20) is read in the Schedule that has it.
-- Units. A day is a whole calendar date; a period in this module is counted in days, weeks (7 days), months and years of the calendar (daydate `add months`, `add years`).
-- The Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954 is not among the sources, so no counting convention of the Law is known: each is a fork (see the field of `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`).

§ `Counting the periods of Schedules 1 and 2`

§§ `F9.1: at least 2 weeks before appointing`

-- Reading A: the notice is in time if the appointment is 14 or more days after the day the notice was presented (the day of the notice is day 0).
-- Reading B: there must be 14 clear days between the two days, so the appointment is 15 or more days after the notice.
-- src:760 | (3) The Minister must, at least 2 weeks before appointing a person as the Commissioner, present to the States a notice of his or her intention to make the appointment.
-- src:818 | (3) The Minister must, at least 2 weeks before appointing a person as a tribunal member, present to the States a notice of his or her intention to make the appointment.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(3); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(3) (src:760) (src:818)
GIVEN `the day of the appointment` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.1 reading A: the latest day for a notice that must precede an event by that many weeks` `the day of the appointment` weeks MEANS
    Date (Day `the day of the appointment` MINUS weeks TIMES 7)

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(3); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(3) (src:760) (src:818)
GIVEN `the day of the appointment` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.1 reading B: the latest day for a notice that leaves that many clear weeks before an event` `the day of the appointment` weeks MEANS
    Date (Day `the day of the appointment` MINUS weeks TIMES 7 MINUS 1)

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(3); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(3) (src:760) (src:818)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day of the appointment` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the latest day for a notice of intention before an appointment` interp `the day of the appointment` weeks MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `the 2 weeks before an appointment`
    WHEN `the appointment is 14 or more days after the notice`
        THEN `F9.1 reading A: the latest day for a notice that must precede an event by that many weeks` `the day of the appointment` weeks
    WHEN `there are 14 clear days between the notice and the appointment`
        THEN `F9.1 reading B: the latest day for a notice that leaves that many clear weeks before an event` `the day of the appointment` weeks

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(3); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(3) (src:760) (src:818)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day of the notice` IS A DATE
      `the day of the appointment` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: a notice of intention was presented in time before an appointment` interp `the day of the notice` `the day of the appointment` weeks MEANS
    `the day of the notice` AT MOST `Sch 1 and 2: the latest day for a notice of intention before an appointment` interp `the day of the appointment` weeks


§§ `F9.14: before appointing, the views of the Jersey Appointments Commission`

-- Reading A: the views were sought on a day EARLIER than the day of the appointment (the Law counts days, so two things on one day cannot be put in order).
-- Reading B: the views were sought on or before the day of the appointment.
-- src:759 | (2) Before appointing a person as the Commissioner, the Minister must seek the views of the Jersey Appointments Commission, established by Article 17 of the Employment of States of Jersey Employees (Jersey) Law 2005, on the appointment (without prejudice to the powers of the States or the Jersey Appointments Commission under Article 15 of that Law).
-- src:817 | (2) Before appointing a person as a tribunal member, the Minister must seek the views of the Jersey Appointments Commission established by Article 17 of the Employment of States of Jersey Employees (Jersey) Law 2005 on the appointment (without prejudice to the powers of the States or the Jersey Appointments Commission under Article 15 of that Law).
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(2); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(2) (src:759) (src:817)
GIVEN `the day of the appointment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.14 reading A: the latest day for the views to be sought is the day before the appointment` `the day of the appointment` MEANS
    Date (Day `the day of the appointment` MINUS 1)

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(2); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(2) (src:759) (src:817)
GIVEN `the day of the appointment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.14 reading B: the latest day for the views to be sought is the day of the appointment` `the day of the appointment` MEANS
    `the day of the appointment`

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(2); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(2) (src:759) (src:817)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day of the appointment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the latest day for the views of the Jersey Appointments Commission to be sought` interp `the day of the appointment` MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `before appointing`
    WHEN `the views were sought on an earlier day than the day of the appointment`
        THEN `F9.14 reading A: the latest day for the views to be sought is the day before the appointment` `the day of the appointment`
    WHEN `the views were sought on or before the day of the appointment`
        THEN `F9.14 reading B: the latest day for the views to be sought is the day of the appointment` `the day of the appointment`

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(2); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(2) (src:759) (src:817)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day on which the views were sought` IS A DATE
      `the day of the appointment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: the views of the Jersey Appointments Commission were sought before an appointment` interp `the day on which the views were sought` `the day of the appointment` MEANS
    `the day on which the views were sought` AT MOST `Sch 1 and 2: the latest day for the views of the Jersey Appointments Commission to be sought` interp `the day of the appointment`


§§ `F9.2: a period of the appointment of not more than 6 years`

-- The start day is the first day of the term. Reading A: the end date is the day the term EXPIRES (3(4)(a): the day on which the person is no longer appointed),
-- so a term may run to the 6th anniversary of its start. Reading B: the end date is the LAST day of the term and is itself a day of the term,
-- so the end date must be before the 6th anniversary. An anniversary of 29 February is 28 February (daydate `add years` clamps).
-- src:762 | (5) When appointing a person as the Commissioner the Minister must determine the period of the appointment, being not more than 6 years.
-- src:820 | (5) When appointing a person as a tribunal member the Minister must determine the period of the appointment, being not more than 6 years.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(5); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(5) (src:762) (src:820)
GIVEN `the first day of the term` IS A DATE
      years IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.2 reading A: the latest end date of a term is the anniversary of its start` `the first day of the term` years MEANS
    `add years` `the first day of the term` years

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(5); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(5) (src:762) (src:820)
GIVEN `the first day of the term` IS A DATE
      years IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.2 reading B: the latest end date of a term is the day before the anniversary of its start` `the first day of the term` years MEANS
    Date (Day (`add years` `the first day of the term` years) MINUS 1)

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(5); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(5) (src:762) (src:820)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the first day of the term` IS A DATE
      years IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the latest end date of a term of an appointment` interp `the first day of the term` years MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `the 6 years of an appointment`
    WHEN `the end date is the day the term expires`
        THEN `F9.2 reading A: the latest end date of a term is the anniversary of its start` `the first day of the term` years
    WHEN `the end date is the last day of the term`
        THEN `F9.2 reading B: the latest end date of a term is the day before the anniversary of its start` `the first day of the term` years

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 1(5); Schedule 2 paragraph 1(5) (src:762) (src:820)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the first day of the term` IS A DATE
      `the end date of the term` IS A DATE
      years IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: a term of an appointment is not more than that many years` interp `the first day of the term` `the end date of the term` years MEANS
    `the end date of the term` AT MOST `Sch 1 and 2: the latest end date of a term of an appointment` interp `the first day of the term` years


§§ `F9.3: resigning by giving not less than a number of months' notice`

-- The notice is given on a day and the resignation takes effect on a day the notice names (the first day on which the person is no longer appointed).
-- Reading A: the office ends when the notice period runs out; the earliest day is the notice day plus the months, the day clamped to the end of the month.
-- Reading B: the office ends on the day the notice is given (the notice must still be for the months, counted as in A).
-- Reading C: as A, but the months are added by rolling: 30 February is 2 March.
-- src:780 | (a) he or she resigns from office by giving not less than 3 months’ notice in writing to the Minister;
-- src:826 | (a) he or she resigns from office by giving not less than one month’s notice in writing to the Minister;
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(1)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(1)(a) (src:780) (src:826)
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.3 reading A: the earliest day a resignation can take effect, the months being clamped` `the day the notice was given` months MEANS
    `add months` `the day the notice was given` months

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(1)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(1)(a) (src:780) (src:826)
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.3 reading B: the earliest day a notice can be for, the office ending when the notice is given` `the day the notice was given` months MEANS
    `add months` `the day the notice was given` months

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(1)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(1)(a) (src:780) (src:826)
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.3 reading C: the earliest day a resignation can take effect, the months being rolled` `the day the notice was given` months MEANS
    Date (DATE_DAY `the day the notice was given`) (DATE_MONTH `the day the notice was given` PLUS months) (DATE_YEAR `the day the notice was given`)

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(1)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(1)(a) (src:780) (src:826)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the earliest day a notice of resignation can name` interp `the day the notice was given` months MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `a resignation on notice`
    WHEN `the office ends when the notice period runs out, the months being clamped`
        THEN `F9.3 reading A: the earliest day a resignation can take effect, the months being clamped` `the day the notice was given` months
    WHEN `the office ends when the notice is given`
        THEN `F9.3 reading B: the earliest day a notice can be for, the office ending when the notice is given` `the day the notice was given` months
    WHEN `the office ends when the notice period runs out, the months being rolled`
        THEN `F9.3 reading C: the earliest day a resignation can take effect, the months being rolled` `the day the notice was given` months

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(1)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(1)(a) (src:780) (src:826)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the day the resignation takes effect` IS A DATE
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: a notice of resignation is for not less than that many months` interp `the day the notice was given` `the day the resignation takes effect` months MEANS
    `the day the resignation takes effect` AT LEAST `Sch 1 and 2: the earliest day a notice of resignation can name` interp `the day the notice was given` months

-- The first day on which the person is no longer appointed because of a resignation, or NOTHING if the notice is too short to be a resignation.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(1)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(1)(a) (src:780) (src:826)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the day the resignation takes effect` IS A DATE
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the day the office ends by resignation` interp `the day the notice was given` `the day the resignation takes effect` months MEANS
    IF `Sch 1 and 2: a notice of resignation is for not less than that many months` interp `the day the notice was given` `the day the resignation takes effect` months
    THEN CONSIDER interp's `a resignation on notice`
         WHEN `the office ends when the notice period runs out, the months being clamped`
             THEN JUST (`F9.3 reading A: the office ends when the notice period runs out` `the day the notice was given` `the day the resignation takes effect`)
         WHEN `the office ends when the notice is given`
             THEN JUST (`F9.3 reading B: the office ends when the notice is given` `the day the notice was given` `the day the resignation takes effect`)
         WHEN `the office ends when the notice period runs out, the months being rolled`
             THEN JUST (`F9.3 reading C: the office ends when the notice period runs out, the months having been rolled` `the day the notice was given` `the day the resignation takes effect`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Where the office ends, on each reading of F9.3, for a notice that is long enough: on the day the notice names (A, C) or on the day the notice is given (B).
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(1)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(1)(a) (src:780) (src:826)
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the day the resignation takes effect` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.3 reading A: the office ends when the notice period runs out` `the day the notice was given` `the day the resignation takes effect` MEANS
    `the day the resignation takes effect`

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(1)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(1)(a) (src:780) (src:826)
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the day the resignation takes effect` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.3 reading B: the office ends when the notice is given` `the day the notice was given` `the day the resignation takes effect` MEANS
    `the day the notice was given`

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(1)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(1)(a) (src:780) (src:826)
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the day the resignation takes effect` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.3 reading C: the office ends when the notice period runs out, the months having been rolled` `the day the notice was given` `the day the resignation takes effect` MEANS
    `the day the resignation takes effect`


§§ `F9.4: an absence for a period longer than a number of consecutive weeks`

-- Reading A: the days of absence are counted, the first day and the last day both included, and compared with 7 times the weeks.
-- Reading B: the elapsed days from the first day to the last day (the later minus the earlier) are compared with 7 times the weeks.
-- src:784 | (a) has been absent from his or her duties for a period longer than 4 consecutive weeks;
-- src:830 | (a) has been absent from his or her duties for a period longer than 8 consecutive weeks;
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(2)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(2)(a) (src:784) (src:830)
GIVEN `the first day of absence` IS A DATE
      `the last day of absence` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.4 reading A: the days of absence, both ends included, are longer than that many weeks` `the first day of absence` `the last day of absence` weeks MEANS
    (Day `the last day of absence` MINUS Day `the first day of absence` PLUS 1) GREATER THAN weeks TIMES 7

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(2)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(2)(a) (src:784) (src:830)
GIVEN `the first day of absence` IS A DATE
      `the last day of absence` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.4 reading B: the elapsed days of absence are longer than that many weeks` `the first day of absence` `the last day of absence` weeks MEANS
    (Day `the last day of absence` MINUS Day `the first day of absence`) GREATER THAN weeks TIMES 7

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(2)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(2)(a) (src:784) (src:830)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the first day of absence` IS A DATE
      `the last day of absence` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: an absence from duties was for a period longer than that many consecutive weeks` interp `the first day of absence` `the last day of absence` weeks MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `an absence from duties`
    WHEN `the days of absence are counted, the first and the last included`
        THEN `F9.4 reading A: the days of absence, both ends included, are longer than that many weeks` `the first day of absence` `the last day of absence` weeks
    WHEN `the elapsed days from the first to the last day of absence are counted`
        THEN `F9.4 reading B: the elapsed days of absence are longer than that many weeks` `the first day of absence` `the last day of absence` weeks


§§ `F9.5: not more than 2 weeks after terminating`

-- Reading A: the notice may be presented up to the 14th day after the termination day (the termination day is day 0).
-- Reading B: the termination day is day 1, so the 13th day after it is the last in time.
-- src:788 | (3) The Minister must, not more than 2 weeks after terminating the appointment, present to the States a notice that the Minister has terminated the appointment.
-- src:834 | (3) The Minister must, not more than 2 weeks after terminating the appointment, present to the States a notice that the Minister has terminated the appointment.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(3); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(3) (src:788) (src:834)
GIVEN `the day of the termination` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.5 reading A: the latest day for a notice after an event is that many weeks after it` `the day of the termination` weeks MEANS
    Date (Day `the day of the termination` PLUS weeks TIMES 7)

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(3); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(3) (src:788) (src:834)
GIVEN `the day of the termination` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.5 reading B: the latest day for a notice after an event is the day before that many weeks after it` `the day of the termination` weeks MEANS
    Date (Day `the day of the termination` PLUS weeks TIMES 7 MINUS 1)

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(3); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(3) (src:788) (src:834)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day of the termination` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the latest day for a notice of a termination` interp `the day of the termination` weeks MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `the 2 weeks after a termination`
    WHEN `the 14th day after the termination day is in time`
        THEN `F9.5 reading A: the latest day for a notice after an event is that many weeks after it` `the day of the termination` weeks
    WHEN `the termination day is day 1, so the 13th day after it is the last in time`
        THEN `F9.5 reading B: the latest day for a notice after an event is the day before that many weeks after it` `the day of the termination` weeks

-- A notice that the Minister has terminated the appointment cannot be presented before the termination, so the notice day is also not before the termination day.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(3); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(3) (src:788) (src:834)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day of the termination` IS A DATE
      `the day of the notice` IS A DATE
      weeks IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: a notice of a termination was presented in time` interp `the day of the termination` `the day of the notice` weeks MEANS
        `the day of the notice` AT LEAST `the day of the termination`
    AND `the day of the notice` AT MOST `Sch 1 and 2: the latest day for a notice of a termination` interp `the day of the termination` weeks


§§ `F9.6 and F9.11: completing 12 years of service, consecutive or in aggregate`

-- A period of service is a stretch of consecutive days. `first day of service` is its first day; `day service stopped` is the first day on which the person was no longer serving
-- (so the days of service are the day service stopped minus the first day of service). The periods are given before the appointment whose expiry is asked about.
DECLARE `Period of service under the Schedules` HAS
    `first day of service` IS A DATE @desc The first day of a period of consecutive service
    `day service stopped` IS A DATE @desc The first day on which the person was no longer serving (the days of service are this day minus the first day of service)
    `as a designated relevant person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc TRUE where the person served in that period as a relevant person designated under Schedule 1 paragraph 4(4), not as the Commissioner appointed

-- F9.11. Reading A: service as a designated relevant person does not count (a designated person is only "treated as the Commissioner", on a temporary basis, 4(5)).
-- Reading B: it counts.
-- src:792 | (c) the person completes 12 years (whether consecutive or in aggregate) of service as Commissioner.
-- src:838 | (c) the person completes 12 years (whether consecutive or in aggregate) of service as a member of the tribunal.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(c); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(c) (src:792) (src:838)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      periods IS A LIST OF `Period of service under the Schedules`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Period of service under the Schedules`
`Sch 1 and 2: the periods of service that count toward twelve years` interp periods MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `service as a designated relevant person`
    WHEN `service as a designated relevant person does not count`
        THEN `F9.11 reading A: service as a designated relevant person is left out` periods
    WHEN `service as a designated relevant person counts`
        THEN `F9.11 reading B: service as a designated relevant person is counted` periods

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(c); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(c) (src:792) (src:838)
GIVEN periods IS A LIST OF `Period of service under the Schedules`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Period of service under the Schedules`
`F9.11 reading A: service as a designated relevant person is left out` periods MEANS
    filter (GIVEN period YIELD NOT (period's `as a designated relevant person`)) periods

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(c); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(c) (src:792) (src:838)
GIVEN periods IS A LIST OF `Period of service under the Schedules`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Period of service under the Schedules`
`F9.11 reading B: service as a designated relevant person is counted` periods MEANS
    periods

GIVEN periods IS A LIST OF `Period of service under the Schedules`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`Sch 1 and 2: the days of service in` periods MEANS
    sum (map (GIVEN period YIELD Day (period's `day service stopped`) MINUS Day (period's `first day of service`)) periods)

GIVEN first IS A DATE
      second IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the earlier of two days` first second MEANS
    IF first AT MOST second THEN first ELSE second

-- The first day of the earliest period, or the fallback if there is none.
GIVEN periods IS A LIST OF `Period of service under the Schedules`
      fallback IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the earliest first day of service in` periods fallback MEANS
    CONSIDER periods
    WHEN EMPTY THEN fallback
    WHEN period FOLLOWED BY rest
        THEN `Sch 1 and 2: the earlier of two days` (period's `first day of service`) (`Sch 1 and 2: the earliest first day of service in` rest fallback)

-- F9.6. Reading A: twelve calendar years, measured in days from the first day of service (the number of days from that day to its 12th anniversary, 4,383 days from 1 March 2026).
-- Reading B: twelve years of 365 days, 4,380 days.
-- src:792 | (c) the person completes 12 years (whether consecutive or in aggregate) of service as Commissioner.
-- src:838 | (c) the person completes 12 years (whether consecutive or in aggregate) of service as a member of the tribunal.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(c); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(c) (src:792) (src:838)
GIVEN `the first day of service` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`F9.6 reading A: the days in twelve calendar years from the first day of service` `the first day of service` MEANS
    Day (`add years` `the first day of service` 12) MINUS Day `the first day of service`

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(c); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(c) (src:792) (src:838)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`F9.6 reading B: the days in twelve years of 365 days` MEANS 12 TIMES 365

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(c); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(c) (src:792) (src:838)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the first day of service` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`Sch 1 and 2: the days of service that make up twelve years` interp `the first day of service` MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `twelve years of service`
    WHEN `twelve calendar years measured in days from the first day of service`
        THEN `F9.6 reading A: the days in twelve calendar years from the first day of service` `the first day of service`
    WHEN `twelve years of 365 days`
        THEN `F9.6 reading B: the days in twelve years of 365 days`

-- The first day on which the person has completed 12 years of service and so is no longer appointed under 3(4)(c): the start of the current appointment plus the days
-- still to be served. If the service before the current appointment already amounts to 12 years, the day is the start itself.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(c); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(c) (src:792) (src:838)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the service before the current appointment` IS A LIST OF `Period of service under the Schedules`
      `the first day of the current appointment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the day on which twelve years of service are completed` interp `the service before the current appointment` `the first day of the current appointment` MEANS
    Date (Day `the first day of the current appointment` PLUS max 0 (target MINUS served))
    WHERE
        counted MEANS `Sch 1 and 2: the periods of service that count toward twelve years` interp `the service before the current appointment`
        served MEANS `Sch 1 and 2: the days of service in` counted
        target MEANS `Sch 1 and 2: the days of service that make up twelve years` interp (`Sch 1 and 2: the earliest first day of service in` counted `the first day of the current appointment`)


§§ `F9.7: not later than 4 months after the end of the year`

-- The year is a calendar year (4(8): "each calendar year"); its end is 31 December. Reading A: the months are added with the day clamped (31 December plus 4 months is 30 April).
-- Reading B: the months are added by rolling (31 April does not exist, so 1 May).
-- src:808 | (9) The Commissioner must provide the Minister with the report as soon as practicable after the end of the year to which the report relates, but in no case later than 4 months after the end of that year.
-- src:843 | (4) The responsible officer must provide the Minister with the report as soon as practicable after the end of the year to which the report relates, but in no case later than 4 months after the end of that year.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) (src:808) (src:843)
GIVEN year IS A NUMBER
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.7 reading A: the latest day for a report on a calendar year, the months being clamped` year months MEANS
    `add months` (YMD year 12 31) months

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) (src:808) (src:843)
GIVEN year IS A NUMBER
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`F9.7 reading B: the latest day for a report on a calendar year, the months being rolled` year months MEANS
    Date 31 (12 PLUS months) year

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) (src:808) (src:843)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      year IS A NUMBER
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the latest day for a report on a calendar year` interp year months MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `the 4 months after the end of the year`
    WHEN `the months are added and the day is clamped to the end of the month`
        THEN `F9.7 reading A: the latest day for a report on a calendar year, the months being clamped` year months
    WHEN `the months are added and the day is rolled into the next month`
        THEN `F9.7 reading B: the latest day for a report on a calendar year, the months being rolled` year months

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) (src:808) (src:843)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      year IS A NUMBER
      `the day on which the report was provided` IS A DATE
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: a report was provided within that many months after the end of the year` interp year `the day on which the report was provided` months MEANS
    `the day on which the report was provided` AT MOST `Sch 1 and 2: the latest day for a report on a calendar year` interp year months


§§ `F9.10: as soon as practicable`

-- Reading A: whether a step was taken as soon as practicable is a judgement of the person who decides, supplied as an input.
-- Reading B: it is measured from the day the step falls due (the end of the year in 4(9); the Minister's receipt of the report in 4(10)) against a number of days (the extra input of the reading).
-- src:808 | (9) The Commissioner must provide the Minister with the report as soon as practicable after the end of the year to which the report relates, but in no case later than 4 months after the end of that year.
-- src:809 | (10) The Minister must lay a copy of the report so provided before the States as soon as practicable after the Minister receives the report.
-- src:843 | (4) The responsible officer must provide the Minister with the report as soon as practicable after the end of the year to which the report relates, but in no case later than 4 months after the end of that year.
-- src:844 | (5) The Minister must lay a copy of the report so provided before the States as soon as practicable after the Minister receives the report.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9) and (10); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) and (5) (src:808-809) (src:843-844)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the step was taken as soon as practicable in the view of the person who decides` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day on which the step became due` IS A DATE
      `the day on which the step was taken` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: a step was taken as soon as practicable` interp `the step was taken as soon as practicable in the view of the person who decides` `the day on which the step became due` `the day on which the step was taken` MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `as soon as practicable`
    WHEN `as soon as practicable is a judgement supplied as an input`
        THEN `F9.10 reading A: a step is as soon as practicable when the person who decides finds it so` `the step was taken as soon as practicable in the view of the person who decides`
    WHEN `as soon as practicable is a number of days from the day the step falls due` limit
        THEN `F9.10 reading B: a step is as soon as practicable when it is taken within that many days of falling due` limit `the day on which the step became due` `the day on which the step was taken`

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9) and (10); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) and (5) (src:808-809) (src:843-844)
GIVEN judgement IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.10 reading A: a step is as soon as practicable when the person who decides finds it so` judgement MEANS
    judgement

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9) and (10); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) and (5) (src:808-809) (src:843-844)
GIVEN limit IS A NUMBER
      `the day on which the step became due` IS A DATE
      `the day on which the step was taken` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.10 reading B: a step is as soon as practicable when it is taken within that many days of falling due` limit `the day on which the step became due` `the day on which the step was taken` MEANS
        `the day on which the step was taken` AT LEAST `the day on which the step became due`
    AND (Day `the day on which the step was taken` MINUS Day `the day on which the step became due`) AT MOST limit


§§ `F9.12: a report for each calendar year`

-- The person held the office from a first day to a last day, both days included. Reading A: a report is required for every calendar year in which the person held the office on at least one day.
-- Reading B: only for a calendar year in which the person held it on every day.
-- src:807 | (8) The Commissioner must prepare and publish a report on the activities of the Commissioner in each calendar year.
-- src:842 | (3) The responsible officer must prepare a report on the activities of the tribunal in each calendar year.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(8); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(3) (src:807) (src:842)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the first day on which the office was held` IS A DATE
      `the last day on which the office was held` IS A DATE
      year IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: a report is required of the holder for a calendar year` interp `the first day on which the office was held` `the last day on which the office was held` year MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `a year in which the office was held for only part of it`
    WHEN `a report is required for the part of the year in which the office was held`
        THEN `F9.12 reading A: a report is required for a year in which the office was held on at least one day` `the first day on which the office was held` `the last day on which the office was held` year
    WHEN `a report is required only for a whole calendar year`
        THEN `F9.12 reading B: a report is required only for a year in which the office was held on every day` `the first day on which the office was held` `the last day on which the office was held` year

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(8); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(3) (src:807) (src:842)
GIVEN `the first day on which the office was held` IS A DATE
      `the last day on which the office was held` IS A DATE
      year IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.12 reading A: a report is required for a year in which the office was held on at least one day` `the first day on which the office was held` `the last day on which the office was held` year MEANS
        `the first day on which the office was held` AT MOST YMD year 12 31
    AND `the last day on which the office was held` AT LEAST YMD year 1 1

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(8); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(3) (src:807) (src:842)
GIVEN `the first day on which the office was held` IS A DATE
      `the last day on which the office was held` IS A DATE
      year IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.12 reading B: a report is required only for a year in which the office was held on every day` `the first day on which the office was held` `the last day on which the office was held` year MEANS
        `the first day on which the office was held` AT MOST YMD year 1 1
    AND `the last day on which the office was held` AT LEAST YMD year 12 31


§§ `F9.17: the person becomes a member of the States`

-- Reading A: only becoming a member on or after the first day of the term ends the appointment. Reading B: being a member at any time during the appointment ends it,
-- so a person who was already a member when appointed has the appointment expire on its first day.
-- src:791 | (b) the person becomes a member of the States; or
-- src:837 | (b) the person becomes a member of the States; or
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(b); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(b) (src:791) (src:837)
GIVEN `the day the person became a member of the States` IS A DATE
      `the first day of the term` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`F9.17 reading A: only becoming a member of the States during the appointment ends it` `the day the person became a member of the States` `the first day of the term` MEANS
    IF `the day the person became a member of the States` AT LEAST `the first day of the term`
    THEN JUST `the day the person became a member of the States`
    ELSE NOTHING

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(b); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(b) (src:791) (src:837)
GIVEN `the day the person became a member of the States` IS A DATE
      `the first day of the term` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`F9.17 reading B: being a member of the States at any time during the appointment ends it` `the day the person became a member of the States` `the first day of the term` MEANS
    JUST (IF `the day the person became a member of the States` AT LEAST `the first day of the term` THEN `the day the person became a member of the States` ELSE `the first day of the term`)

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(4)(b); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(4)(b) (src:791) (src:837)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day the person became a member of the States` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the first day of the term` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the day on which membership of the States ends the appointment` interp `the day the person became a member of the States` `the first day of the term` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the day the person became a member of the States`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST member
        THEN CONSIDER interp's `membership of the States when appointed`
             WHEN `only becoming a member during the appointment ends it`
                 THEN `F9.17 reading A: only becoming a member of the States during the appointment ends it` member `the first day of the term`
             WHEN `being a member at any time during the appointment ends it`
                 THEN `F9.17 reading B: being a member of the States at any time during the appointment ends it` member `the first day of the term`


§§ `F9.19: the ground of absence for terminating an appointment`

-- The Minister may terminate "after satisfying himself or herself that the person has been absent from his or her duties for a period longer than" a number of consecutive weeks.
-- Reading A: both the Minister's satisfaction and an absence that really is longer than the number of weeks are required. Reading B: the Minister's satisfaction alone is required.
-- src:784 | (a) has been absent from his or her duties for a period longer than 4 consecutive weeks;
-- src:830 | (a) has been absent from his or her duties for a period longer than 8 consecutive weeks;
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(2)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(2)(a) (src:784) (src:830)
GIVEN `the Minister is satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the absence is longer than the number of weeks` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.19 reading A: the ground of absence needs the Minister's satisfaction and an absence of the length` `the Minister is satisfied` `the absence is longer than the number of weeks` MEANS
        `the Minister is satisfied`
    AND `the absence is longer than the number of weeks`

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(2)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(2)(a) (src:784) (src:830)
GIVEN `the Minister is satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the absence is longer than the number of weeks` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.19 reading B: the ground of absence needs the Minister's satisfaction alone` `the Minister is satisfied` `the absence is longer than the number of weeks` MEANS
    `the Minister is satisfied`

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 3(2)(a); Schedule 2 paragraph 2(2)(a) (src:784) (src:830)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the Minister is satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the absence is longer than the number of weeks` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: the ground of absence for terminating an appointment is made out` interp `the Minister is satisfied` `the absence is longer than the number of weeks` MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `the ground of absence for terminating an appointment`
    WHEN `the Minister's satisfaction and the absence longer than the number of weeks are both required`
        THEN `F9.19 reading A: the ground of absence needs the Minister's satisfaction and an absence of the length` `the Minister is satisfied` `the absence is longer than the number of weeks`
    WHEN `the Minister's satisfaction alone is required`
        THEN `F9.19 reading B: the ground of absence needs the Minister's satisfaction alone` `the Minister is satisfied` `the absence is longer than the number of weeks`


§§ `The text of Schedule 1 that carries the Dormant Bank Accounts words (F9.9, trap T22)`

-- The words "subject to Article 20(1) of the Dormant Bank Accounts (Jersey) Law 2017" were added to paragraphs 2(4)(a) and 4(7) by L.1/2017 Article 30(3) (endnote [5]).
-- The Table of Legislation History gives their commencement as 16 December 2019; the Act that appointed that day is not among the sources.
-- The consolidated versions begin on 1 January 2019, so the text of 1 January 2019 and of 31 May 2019 lacks the words and every later text has them;
-- no text before 1 January 2019 is among the sources, so that vintage answers with a refusal.
-- src:776 | (a) subject to Article 20(1) of the Dormant Bank Accounts (Jersey) Law 2017, the remuneration or other payment for services of the Commissioner, due under the terms of his or her appointment, must be paid out of the annual income of the States; and
-- src:806 | (7) Subject to Article 20(1) of the Dormant Bank Accounts (Jersey) Law 2017, the cost of providing staff, accommodation and equipment under this paragraph must be met out of the annual income of the States.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraphs 2(4)(a) and 4(7), as amended by L.1/2017 Article 30(3) (src:776) (src:806)
GIVEN `the vintage of the text` IS A `Vintage of the text` @desc Which of the nine consolidated texts of the Law is read (decided by the temporal group)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 1 and 2: the Dormant Bank Accounts words are in the text of` `the vintage of the text` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the vintage of the text`
    WHEN `before the first consolidated version`
        THEN REFUSE "the sources hold no text of Schedule 1 before 1 January 2019, so they do not say whether it then carried the Dormant Bank Accounts words"
    WHEN `the text of 1 January 2019` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the text of 31 May 2019` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the text of 16 December 2019` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the text of 1 January 2020` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the text of 2 March 2021` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the text of 12 April 2023` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the text of 27 February 2024` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the text of 16 October 2025` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the text of 2 October 2026` THEN TRUE


§§ `Optional days`

-- The earlier of two days that may be absent: a day that is absent does not count. NOTHING if both are absent.
GIVEN first IS A MAYBE DATE
      second IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the earlier of two optional days` first second MEANS
    CONSIDER first
    WHEN NOTHING THEN second
    WHEN JUST one THEN CONSIDER second
                       WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST one
                       WHEN JUST other THEN JUST (`Sch 1 and 2: the earlier of two days` one other)


§§ `F9.10 continued: the last day of a step that is due as soon as practicable`

-- Under reading A "as soon as practicable" fixes no day (it is a judgement), so there is NOTHING to put in a deadline. Under reading B it is the day the step falls due plus the number of days.
-- src:808 | (9) The Commissioner must provide the Minister with the report as soon as practicable after the end of the year to which the report relates, but in no case later than 4 months after the end of that year.
-- src:809 | (10) The Minister must lay a copy of the report so provided before the States as soon as practicable after the Minister receives the report.
-- src:843 | (4) The responsible officer must provide the Minister with the report as soon as practicable after the end of the year to which the report relates, but in no case later than 4 months after the end of that year.
-- src:844 | (5) The Minister must lay a copy of the report so provided before the States as soon as practicable after the Minister receives the report.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9) and (10); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) and (5) (src:808-809) (src:843-844)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day on which the step falls due` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the last day for a step that is to be taken as soon as practicable` interp `the day on which the step falls due` MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `as soon as practicable`
    WHEN `as soon as practicable is a judgement supplied as an input` THEN `F9.10 reading A: a judgement fixes no last day`
    WHEN `as soon as practicable is a number of days from the day the step falls due` limit
        THEN `F9.10 reading B: the last day is that many days after the day the step falls due` limit `the day on which the step falls due`

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9) and (10); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) and (5) (src:808-809) (src:843-844)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`F9.10 reading A: a judgement fixes no last day` MEANS NOTHING

@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9) and (10); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) and (5) (src:808-809) (src:843-844)
GIVEN limit IS A NUMBER
      `the day on which the step falls due` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`F9.10 reading B: the last day is that many days after the day the step falls due` limit `the day on which the step falls due` MEANS
    JUST (Date (Day `the day on which the step falls due` PLUS limit))

-- A step that must be taken as soon as practicable but in no case later than a day: the day it must be taken by is the earlier of the two.
@ref Schedule 1 paragraph 4(9); Schedule 2 paragraph 3(4) (src:808) (src:843)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      `the day on which the step falls due` IS A DATE
      `the day after which the step is in no case in time` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`Sch 1 and 2: the last day for a step that is to be taken as soon as practicable and in no case after a day` interp `the day on which the step falls due` `the day after which the step is in no case in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `Sch 1 and 2: the last day for a step that is to be taken as soon as practicable` interp `the day on which the step falls due`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the day after which the step is in no case in time`
    WHEN JUST sooner THEN `Sch 1 and 2: the earlier of two days` sooner `the day after which the step is in no case in time`
